1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,176
Here's a scenario
every support engineer knows.

2
00:00:03,426 --> 00:00:05,993
A customer has waited weeks for a fix.

3
00:00:06,243 --> 00:00:11,446
Engineering closes the ticket, and you
write back: "Good news, it's shipped."

4
00:00:11,696 --> 00:00:14,601
Ten minutes later:
"We still can't see it."

5
00:00:14,851 --> 00:00:18,703
It was built. It was never
switched on for their account.

6
00:00:18,953 --> 00:00:22,128
The ticket was closed.
The promise wasn't kept.

7
00:00:22,628 --> 00:00:27,102
Promise Ledger catches that.
Here's Northstar, a fictional customer.

8
00:00:27,352 --> 00:00:29,352
I run the evidence check.

9
00:00:29,602 --> 00:00:34,339
Each step streams in live: the model,
the time and every quote it cites.

10
00:00:37,089 --> 00:00:41,168
Three NVIDIA Nemotron models
on Nebius Token Factory did that.

11
00:00:41,418 --> 00:00:43,786
Nemotron 3 Nano triaged the sources.

12
00:00:44,036 --> 00:00:48,049
Nemotron 3 Super pulled out every
promise, with exact quotes.

13
00:00:48,299 --> 00:00:52,576
Our rules decided the verdict,
and Nemotron 3 Ultra explained it.

14
00:00:52,826 --> 00:00:54,826
The verdict: delivery gap.

15
00:00:55,076 --> 00:00:59,681
Built? Yes. Enabled for Northstar? No.
Here's the proof, side by side.

16
00:00:59,931 --> 00:01:05,392
Tavily fetched our public changelog live:
"generally available." Not for Northstar.

17
00:01:05,642 --> 00:01:10,115
Ultra drafts an honest update,
and nudges the person who can fix it.

18
00:01:10,365 --> 00:01:12,365
I edit it and approve it.

19
00:01:13,815 --> 00:01:16,249
Then export.
Nothing sends by itself.

20
00:01:17,499 --> 00:01:20,986
Now access is on and the test passed.
I run it again.

21
00:01:24,936 --> 00:01:30,134
But an error-monitoring provider shows
it failing for them: 14 events, 3 users.

22
00:01:30,384 --> 00:01:32,384
So: needs verification.

23
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:35,868
Now the clean scenario.
One more run.

24
00:01:39,618 --> 00:01:43,368
With no open errors for them,
the acceptance test stands:

25
00:01:43,618 --> 00:01:46,842
verified delivered.
Because the evidence changed.

26
00:01:47,342 --> 00:01:52,144
It's for support engineers, customer-
success managers and account teams:

27
00:01:52,394 --> 00:01:55,420
anyone who has to tell
a customer "it's done."

28
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:59,420
I lead technical support at
an error-monitoring provider.

29
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:02,434
This is the check I wish
every update had.

30
00:02:02,934 --> 00:02:08,318
Under the hood, all three are NVIDIA
Nemotron models on Nebius Token Factory,

31
00:02:08,568 --> 00:02:11,435
served through one
OpenAI-compatible API.

32
00:02:11,685 --> 00:02:14,413
One key, pay per token,
no GPUs to run.

33
00:02:14,663 --> 00:02:18,089
Every step shows the real model,
tokens and cost.

34
00:02:18,339 --> 00:02:22,675
A full run takes under twenty seconds
and costs about a cent.

35
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:27,750
In our evaluation, the full pipeline
matched 9 of 9 development cases

36
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,448
and 31 of 32 frozen held-out cases.

37
00:02:30,698 --> 00:02:33,914
Small synthetic tests,
not a production claim.

38
00:02:34,414 --> 00:02:36,849
A closed ticket isn't a kept promise.

39
00:02:37,099 --> 00:02:40,717
Promise Ledger is the check
before you say "delivered."
